+L24: # Containers in Azure Container Apps
+L25: 
+L26: Feedback
+L27: 
+L28: Summarize this article for me
+L29: ## In this article
+L30: 
+L31: Azure Container Apps manages the details of Kubernetes and container orchestration for you. Containers in Azure Container Apps can use any runtime, programming language, or development stack of your choice.
+L32: 
+L33: cite5†Image: Azure Container Apps: Containers L34: 
+L35: Azure Container Apps supports:
+L36: 
+L37:   * Any Linux-based x86-64 (`linux/amd64`) container image
+L38:   * Containers from any public or private container registry
+L39:   * Optional cite6†sidecar and cite7†init containers
+L40: 
+L41: Features also include:
+L42:   * Apps use the `template` configuration section to define the container image and other settings. Changes to the `template` configuration section trigger a new cite8†container app revision .
+L43:   * If a container crashes, it automatically restarts.
+L44: 
+L45: Jobs features include:
+L46:   * Job executions use the `template` configuration section to define the container image and other settings when each execution starts.
+L47:   * If a container exits with a non-zero exit code, the job execution is marked as failed. You can configure a job to retry failed executions.
+L48: ## Configuration
+L49: Most container apps have a single container. In advanced scenarios, an app can also have sidecar and init containers. In a container app definition, the main app and its sidecar containers are listed in the `containers` array in the cite9†`properties.template` section, and init containers are listed in the `initContainers` array. The following excerpt shows the available configuration options when setting up an app's containers.
+L50: 
+L51:     `{
+L52:       "properties": {
+L53:         "template": {
+L54:           "containers": [
+L55:             {
+L56:               "name": "main",
+L57:               "image": "[parameters('container_image')]",
+L58:               "env": [
+L59:                 {
+L60:                   "name": "HTTP_PORT",
+L61:                   "value": "80"
+L62:                 },
+L63:                 {
+L64:                   "name": "SECRET_VAL",
+L65:                   "secretRef": "mysecret"
+L66:                 }
+L67:               ],
+L68:               "resources": {
+L69:                 "cpu": 0.5,
+L70:                 "memory": "1Gi"
+L71:               },
+L72:               "volumeMounts": [
+L73:                 {
+L74:                   "mountPath": "/appsettings",
+L75:                   "volumeName": "appsettings-volume"
+L76:                 }
+L77:               ],
+L78:               "probes": [
+L79:                 {
+L80:                   "type": "liveness",
+L81:                   "httpGet": {
+L82:                     "path": "/health",
+L83:                     "port": 8080,
+L84:                     "httpHeaders": [
+L85:                       {
+L86:                         "name": "Custom-Header",
+L87:                         "value": "liveness probe"
+L88:                       }
+L89:                     ]
+L90:                   },
+L91:                   "initialDelaySeconds": 7,
+L92:                   "periodSeconds": 3
+L93:                 },
+L94:                 {
+L95:                   "type": "readiness",
+L96:                   "tcpSocket": {
+L97:                     "port": 8081
+L98:                   },
+L99:                   "initialDelaySeconds": 10,
+L100:                   "periodSeconds": 3
+L101:                 },
+L102:                 {
+L103:                   "type": "startup",
+L104:                   "httpGet": {
+L105:                     "path": "/startup",
+L106:                     "port": 8080,
+L107:                     "httpHeaders": [
+L108:                       {
+L109:                         "name": "Custom-Header",
+L110:                         "value": "startup probe"
+L111:                       }
+L112:                     ]
+L113:                   },
+L114:                   "initialDelaySeconds": 3,
+L115:                   "periodSeconds": 3
+L116:                 }
+L117:               ]
+L118:             }
+L119:           ]
+L120:         },
+L121:         "initContainers": [
+L122:           {
+L123:             "name": "init",
+L124:             "image": "[parameters('init_container_image')]",
+L125:             "resources": {
+L126:               "cpu": 0.25,
+L127:               "memory": "0.5Gi"
+L128:             },
+L129:             "volumeMounts": [
+L130:               {
+L131:                 "mountPath": "/appsettings",
+L132:                 "volumeName": "appsettings-volume"
+L133:               }
+L134:             ]
+L135:           }
+L136:         ]
+L137:         ...
+L138:       }
+L139:       ...
+L140:     }
+L141:     `
+L142: Setting  | Description  | Remarks
+L143: --- | --- | ---
+L144: `image`  | The container image name for your container app.  | This value takes the form of `repository/<IMAGE_NAME>:<TAG>`.
+L145: Avoid using static tags like `latest` for container images. Using static tags can lead to caching problems and can make your app difficult to troubleshoot. Instead, use unique tags for each deployment, such as a Git hash or date and time to ensure that updates are properly tracked and deployed.
+L146: `name`  | Friendly name of the container.  | Used for reporting and identification.
+L147: `command`  | The container's startup command.  | Equivalent to Docker's cite10†entrypoint†docs.docker.com field.
+L148: `args`  | Start up command arguments.  | Entries in the array are joined together to create a parameter list to pass to the startup command.
+L149: `env`  | An array of name/value pairs that define environment variables.  | Use `secretRef` instead of the `value` field when referencing a secret.
+L150: `resources.cpu`  | The number of CPUs allocated to the container.  | See cite11†vCPU and memory allocation requirements L151: `resources.memory`  | The amount of RAM allocated to the container.  | See cite11†vCPU and memory allocation requirements L152: `volumeMounts`  | An array of volume mount definitions.  | You can define a temporary or permanent storage volumes for your container. For more information about storage volumes, see cite12†Use storage mounts in Azure Container Apps .
+L153: `probes`  | An array of health probes enabled in the container.  | For more information about probes settings, see cite13†Health probes in Azure Container Apps .
+L154: ### vCPU and memory allocation requirements
+L155: 
+L156: When you use the Consumption plan, the total CPU and memory allocated to all the containers in a container app must add up to one of the following combinations.
+L157: vCPUs (cores)  | Memory
+L158: --- | ---
+L159: `0.25`  | `0.5Gi`
+L160: `0.5`  | `1.0Gi`
+L161: `0.75`  | `1.5Gi`
+L162: `1.0`  | `2.0Gi`
+L163: `1.25`  | `2.5Gi`
+L164: `1.5`  | `3.0Gi`
+L165: `1.75`  | `3.5Gi`
+L166: `2.0`  | `4.0Gi`
+L167: `2.25`  | `4.5Gi`
+L168: `2.5`  | `5.0Gi`
+L169: `2.75`  | `5.5Gi`
+L170: `3.0`  | `6.0Gi`
+L171: `3.25`  | `6.5Gi`
+L172: `3.5`  | `7.0Gi`
+L173: `3.75`  | `7.5Gi`
+L174: `4.0`  | `8.0Gi`
+L175: 
+L176: Note
+L177: 
+L178: Apps using the Consumption plan in a Consumption only environment are limited to a maximum of 2 cores and 4Gi of memory.
+L179: ## Multiple containers
+L180: 
+L181: In advanced scenarios, you can run multiple containers in a single container app. Use this pattern only in specific instances where your containers are tightly coupled.
+L182: 
+L183: For most microservice scenarios, the best practice is to deploy each service as a separate container app.
+L184: 
+L185: Multiple containers in the same container app share hard disk and network resources and experience the same cite8†application lifecycle .
+

