+L24: # Manage data retention in a Log Analytics workspace
+L25: 
+L26: Feedback
+L27: 
+L28: Summarize this article for me
+L29: ## In this article
+L30: 
+L31: A Log Analytics workspace retains data in two states:
+L32: 
+L33:   * Analytics retention: In this state, data is available for monitoring, troubleshooting, and near-real-time analytics.
+L34:   * Long-term retention: In this low-cost state, Analytics and Basic data is available through cite5†search jobs . Auxiliary data remains queryable throughout its total retention period.
+L35: 
+L36: This article explains how Log Analytics workspaces retain data and how to manage the data retention of tables in your workspace.
+L37: ## Analytics, long-term, and total retention
+L38: 
+L39: By default, all tables in a Log Analytics workspace retain data for 30 days, except for cite6†log tables with 90-day default retention . Tables with the Analytics plan make your data available for real-time queries during this Analytics retention period. All table plans can retrieve the stored data through queries or search jobs, and the data is available for visualizations, alerts, and other features and services, based on the table plan.
+L40: Extend the analytics retention period of tables with the Analytics plan up to two years. Basic plan tables have a fixed period of 30 days for queries while Auxiliary plan tables can be queried for the total retention period. Both Basic and Auxiliary tables have additional considerations however. For more information, see cite7†Query data in Basic and Auxiliary tables .
+L41: 
+L42: Note
+L43: You can reduce the analytics retention period of Analytics tables to as little as four days using the API or CLI. However, since 31 days of analytics retention are included in the ingestion price, lowering the retention period below 31 days doesn't reduce costs.
+L44: To retain data in the same table beyond the default retention period, extend the table's total retention to up to 12 years. Analytics data remains in the table after Analytics retention for the remainder of the configured total retention period. Run a search job to retrieve Analytics data after Analytics retention or Basic data older than 30 days when the query doesn't include an Auxiliary table. Auxiliary data remains queryable throughout its total retention period.
+L45: ## How retention modifications work
+L46: 
+L47: When you shorten a table's total retention, Azure Monitor Logs waits 30 days before removing the data, so you can revert the change and avoid data loss if you made an error in configuration.
+L48: 
+L49: When you increase total retention, the new retention period applies to all data that was already ingested into the table and wasn't yet removed.
+L50: 
+L51: When you change the long-term retention settings of a table with existing data, the change takes effect immediately.
+L52: 
+L53: Example:
+L54:   * You have an existing Analytics table with 180 days of analytics retention and no long-term retention.
+L55:   * You change the analytics retention to 90 days without changing the total retention period of 180 days.
+L56:   * Azure Monitor automatically treats the remaining 90 days of total retention as low-cost, long-term retention, so that data that's 90-180 days old isn't lost.
+L57: ## Permissions required
+L58: Action  | Permissions required
+L59: --- | ---
+L60: Configure default analytics retention for Analytics tables in a Log Analytics workspace  | `Microsoft.OperationalInsights/workspaces/write` and `microsoft.operationalinsights/workspaces/tables/write` permissions to the Log Analytics workspace, as provided by the cite8†Log Analytics Contributor built-in role , for example
+L61: Get retention setting by table for a Log Analytics workspace  | `Microsoft.OperationalInsights/workspaces/tables/read` permissions to the Log Analytics workspace, as provided by the cite9†Log Analytics Reader built-in role , for example
+L62: ## Configure the default analytics retention period of Analytics tables
+L63: 
+L64: The default retention period of Analytics tables in a Log Analytics workspace is 30 days. Change the default analytics period of Analytics tables up to two years by modifying the workspace-level data retention setting. Basic and Auxiliary tables only have a total retention period, which is 30 days by default.
+L65: Changing the default workspace-level data retention setting automatically affects all Analytics tables to which the default setting still applies in your workspace. If you've already changed the analytics retention of a particular table, that table isn't affected when you change the workspace default data retention setting.
+L66: 
+L67: Important
+L68: Workspaces with 30-day retention might keep data for 31 days. If you need to retain data for 30 days only to comply with a privacy policy, configure the default workspace retention to 30 days by using the API and update the `immediatePurgeDataOn30Days` workspace property to `true`. Use the cite10†Workspaces - Update API for this operation.
+L69: 
+L70: The following examples set the workspace's default analytics retention to 30 days. The REST example also enables immediate removal after 30 days.
+L71:   * cite11†Portal L72:   * cite12†Azure CLI L73:   * cite13†Azure PowerShell L74:   * cite14†REST L75:   * cite15†Bicep L76:   * cite16†ARM template L77: 
+L78: To set the default analytics retention period of Analytics tables within a Log Analytics workspace:
+L79: 
+L80:   1. From the Log Analytics workspaces menu in the Azure portal, select your workspace.
+L81: 
+L82:   2. In the Settings section, select Usage and estimated costs in the left pane.
+L83: 
+L84:   3. Select Data Retention at the top of the page.
+L85:   4. Move the slider to increase or decrease the number of days, and then select OK.
+L86: The following Azure CLI example uses the cite17†`az monitor log-analytics workspace update` command. It sets the default analytics retention period by using the `--retention-time` parameter.
+L87: 
+L88:     `# Set variables
+L89:     resourceGroupName="<ResourceGroupName>"
+L90:     workspaceName="<WorkspaceName>"
+L91:     retentionInDays="30"
+L92: 
+L93:     # Update the default workspace retention
+L94:     az monitor log-analytics workspace update \
+L95:       --resource-group "$resourceGroupName" \
+L96:       --workspace-name "$workspaceName" \
+L97:       --retention-time "$retentionInDays"
+L98:     `
+L99: Note
+L100: 
+L101: Azure CLI commands use the Azure Resource Manager endpoint from the current CLI context, so management.azure.com doesn't need to be specified in the command syntax.
+L102: The following Azure PowerShell example uses the cite18†`Set-AzOperationalInsightsWorkspace` cmdlet. It sets the default analytics retention period by using the `-RetentionInDays` parameter.
+L103: 
+L104:     `# Set variables
+L105:     $resourceGroupName = "<ResourceGroupName>"
+L106:     $workspaceName = "<WorkspaceName>"
+L107:     $retentionInDays = 30
+L108: 
+L109:     # Define parameters for Set-AzOperationalInsightsWorkspace
+L110:     $setAzOperationalInsightsWorkspaceParams = @{
+L111:         ResourceGroupName = $resourceGroupName
+L112:         Name              = $workspaceName
+L113:         RetentionInDays   = $retentionInDays
+L114:     }
+L115: 
+L116:     # Update the default workspace retention
+L117:     Set-AzOperationalInsightsWorkspace @setAzOperationalInsightsWorkspaceParams
+L118:     `
+L119: Note
+L120: 
+L121: Azure PowerShell cmdlets use the Azure Resource Manager endpoint from the current Az context, so management.azure.com doesn’t need to be specified in the cmdlet syntax.
+L122: 
+L123: The following REST example uses the cite10†`Workspaces - Update` REST API operation. It sets the default analytics retention period for Analytics tables in a Log Analytics workspace.
+L124: It also ensures that data is immediately removed after 30 days and is nonrecoverable.
+L125: 
+L126:     `PATCH https://management.azure.com/subscriptions/{subscriptionId}/resourceGroups/{resourceGroupName}/providers/Microsoft.OperationalInsights/workspaces/{workspaceName}?api-version={apiVersion}
+L127:     Authorization: Bearer {accessToken}
+L128:     Content-Type: application/json
+L129: 
+L130:     {
+L131:       "properties": {
+L132:         "retentionInDays": 30,
+L133:         "features": {
+L134:           "immediatePurgeDataOn30Days": true
+L135:         }
+L136:       }
+L137:     }
+L138:     `
+L139: Request body properties:
+L140: 
+L141: The request body includes the values in the following table.
+L142: 
+L143: Name  | Type  | Description
+L144: --- | --- | ---
+L145: `properties.retentionInDays`  | integer  | The workspace data retention in days. Allowed values are per pricing plan. See pricing tiers documentation for details.
+L146: `properties.features.immediatePurgeDataOn30Days`  | boolean  | Flag that indicates whether data is immediately removed after 30 days and is nonrecoverable. Applicable only when workspace retention is set to 30 days.
+L147: Response example:
+L148: 
+L149: Status code: 200
+L150: 
+L151:     `{
+L152:       "properties": {
+L153:         "retentionInDays": 30,
+L154:         "features": {
+L155:           "legacy": 0,
+L156:           "searchVersion": 1,
+L157:           "immediatePurgeDataOn30Days": true
+L158:         }
+L159:       }
+L160:     }
+L161:     `
+L162: 
+L163: Note
+L164: 
+L165: Bicep deployments are create-or-update operations, not partial PATCH operations. For a retention-only update, use the Azure CLI, Azure PowerShell, or REST tab.
+L166: The following Bicep example uses the cite19†`Microsoft.OperationalInsights/workspaces` resource type.
+L167: 
+L168: This template includes the workspace resource declaration and its default retention setting. Before using it to update an existing workspace:
+L169:   * Set `workspaceName` and `azureRegion` to the existing workspace's name and region.
+L170:   * Use the `retentionInDays` parameter to set `properties.retentionInDays` to `30`. Don't replace the existing `properties` object with only this member.
+L171:   * Retain the workspace's identity, SKU, location, tags, network settings, all other properties, and existing array entries from your maintained definition.
+L172:   * Leave `properties.features.immediatePurgeDataOn30Days` unchanged. The REST tab shows the separate immediate-removal behavior.
+L173:   * Don't deploy this example unchanged over an existing workspace. It shows the retention setting, not your workspace's complete configuration.
+L174: 
+L175:     `param workspaceName string = '<WorkspaceName>'
+L176:     param azureRegion string = '<AzureRegion>'
+L177:     param retentionInDays int = 30
+L178:     resource logAnalyticsWorkspace 'Microsoft.OperationalInsights/workspaces@<ApiVersion>' = {
+L179:       name: workspaceName
+L180:       location: azureRegion
+L181:       properties: {
+L182:         retentionInDays: retentionInDays
+L183:       }
+L184:     }
+L185:     `
+L186: Note
+L187: 
+L188: ARM template deployments are create-or-update operations, not partial PATCH operations. For a retention-only update, use the Azure CLI, Azure PowerShell, or REST tab.
+L189: 
+L190: The following ARM template example uses the cite20†`Microsoft.OperationalInsights/workspaces` resource type.
+L191: 
+L192: This template includes the workspace resource declaration and its default retention setting. Before using it to update an existing workspace:
+L193:   * Set `workspaceName` and `azureRegion` to the existing workspace's name and region.
+L194:   * Use the `retentionInDays` parameter to set `properties.retentionInDays` to `30`. Don't replace the existing `properties` object with only this member.
+L195:   * Retain the workspace's identity, SKU, location, tags, network settings, all other properties, and existing array entries from your maintained definition.
+L196:   * Leave `properties.features.immediatePurgeDataOn30Days` unchanged. The REST tab shows the separate immediate-removal behavior.
+L197:   * Don't deploy this example unchanged over an existing workspace. It shows the retention setting, not your workspace's complete configuration.
+L198: 
+L199:     `{
+L200:       "$schema": "https://schema.management.azure.com/schemas/2019-04-01/deploymentTemplate.json#",
