+L229: ## Configure table-level retention
+L230: 
+L231: By default, all tables with the Analytics data plan inherit the cite21†Log Analytics workspace's default retention setting and have no long-term retention. You can increase the analytics retention period of Analytics tables to up to 730 days at an cite22†extra cost†azure.microsoft.com .
+L232: 
+L233: To add long-term retention to a table with any data plan, set total retention to up to 12 years (4,383 days).
+L234: 
+L235: Note
+L236: The Azure CLI `--total-retention-time` parameter supports up to 2,556 days (seven years). For longer retention, use the Azure portal, REST API, or a version of cite23†`Update-AzOperationalInsightsTable` that supports the requested value. The PowerShell reference lists values through 4,383 days.
+L237: The following examples set an Analytics table's analytics retention to 30 days and total retention to two years (730 days), leaving 700 days of long-term retention. For Basic and Auxiliary tables, set only total retention; `retentionInDays` is read-only.
+L238: 
+L239:   * cite24†Portal L240:   * cite25†Azure CLI L241:   * cite26†Azure PowerShell L242:   * cite27†REST L243:   * cite28†Bicep L244:   * cite29†ARM template L245: 
+L246: To modify the retention setting for a table in the Azure portal:
+L247: 
+L248:   1. From the Log Analytics workspaces menu, select Tables.
+L249: The Tables screen lists all the tables in the workspace.
+L250: 
+L251:   2. Select the context menu for the table you want to configure and select Manage table.
+L252: 
+L253:   3. Configure the analytics retention and total retention settings in the Data retention settings section of the table configuration screen.
+L254: 
+L255: The following Azure CLI example uses the cite30†`az monitor log-analytics workspace table update` command. It modifies a table's retention settings by using the `--retention-time` and `--total-retention-time` parameters.
+L256: Set custom retention:
+L257: 
+L258:     `# Set variables
+L259:     resourceGroupName="<ResourceGroupName>"
+L260:     workspaceName="<WorkspaceName>"
+L261:     tableName="<TableName>"
+L262:     retentionInDays="30"
+L263:     totalRetentionInDays="730"
+L264: 
+L265:     # Update the table retention settings
+L266:     az monitor log-analytics workspace table update \
+L267:       --resource-group "$resourceGroupName" \
+L268:       --workspace-name "$workspaceName" \
+L269:       --name "$tableName" \
+L270:       --retention-time "$retentionInDays" \
+L271:       --total-retention-time "$totalRetentionInDays"
+L272:     `
+L273: Reset to workspace defaults:
+L274: To inherit the workspace's analytics retention and set total retention to the same period, set both `--retention-time` and `--total-retention-time` to `-1`. This removes the table's separate long-term retention period; it doesn't set total retention to zero.
+L275: 
+L276:     `# Set variables
+L277:     resourceGroupName="<ResourceGroupName>"
+L278:     workspaceName="<WorkspaceName>"
+L279:     tableName="<TableName>"
+L280:     retentionInDays="-1"
+L281:     totalRetentionInDays="-1"
+L282: 
+L283:     # Update the table retention settings
+L284:     az monitor log-analytics workspace table update \
+L285:       --resource-group "$resourceGroupName" \
+L286:       --workspace-name "$workspaceName" \
+L287:       --name "$tableName" \
+L288:       --retention-time "$retentionInDays" \
+L289:       --total-retention-time "$totalRetentionInDays"
+L290:     `
+L291: Note
+L292: 
+L293: Azure CLI commands use the Azure Resource Manager endpoint from the current CLI context, so management.azure.com doesn't need to be specified in the command syntax.
+L294: 
+L295: The following Azure PowerShell example uses the cite23†`Update-AzOperationalInsightsTable` cmdlet. It modifies a table's retention settings by using the `-RetentionInDays` and `-TotalRetentionInDays` parameters.
+L296: Set custom retention:
+L297: 
+L298:     `# Set variables
+L299:     $resourceGroupName = "<ResourceGroupName>"
+L300:     $workspaceName = "<WorkspaceName>"
+L301:     $tableName = "<TableName>"
+L302:     $retentionInDays = 30
+L303:     $totalRetentionInDays = 730
+L304: 
+L305:     # Define parameters for Update-AzOperationalInsightsTable
+L306:     $updateAzOperationalInsightsTableParams = @{
+L307:         ResourceGroupName    = $resourceGroupName
+L308:         WorkspaceName        = $workspaceName
+L309:         TableName            = $tableName
+L310:         RetentionInDays      = $retentionInDays
+L311:         TotalRetentionInDays = $totalRetentionInDays
+L312:     }
+L313: 
+L314:     # Update the table retention settings
+L315:     Update-AzOperationalInsightsTable @updateAzOperationalInsightsTableParams
+L316:     `
+L317: Reset to workspace defaults:
+L318: To inherit the workspace's analytics retention and set total retention to the same period, set both `-RetentionInDays` and `-TotalRetentionInDays` to `-1`. This removes the table's separate long-term retention period; it doesn't set total retention to zero.
+L319: 
+L320:     `# Set variables
+L321:     $resourceGroupName = "<ResourceGroupName>"
+L322:     $workspaceName = "<WorkspaceName>"
+L323:     $tableName = "<TableName>"
+L324:     $retentionInDays = -1
+L325:     $totalRetentionInDays = -1
+L326:     # Define parameters for Update-AzOperationalInsightsTable
+L327:     $updateAzOperationalInsightsTableParams = @{
+L328:         ResourceGroupName    = $resourceGroupName
+L329:         WorkspaceName        = $workspaceName
+L330:         TableName            = $tableName
+L331:         RetentionInDays      = $retentionInDays
+L332:         TotalRetentionInDays = $totalRetentionInDays
+L333:     }
+L334: 
+L335:     # Update the table retention settings
+L336:     Update-AzOperationalInsightsTable @updateAzOperationalInsightsTableParams
+L337:     `
+L338: Note
+L339: 
+L340: Azure PowerShell cmdlets use the Azure Resource Manager endpoint from the current Az context, so management.azure.com doesn’t need to be specified in the cmdlet syntax.
+L341: 
+L342: The following REST example uses the cite31†`Tables - Update` REST API operation. It modifies the retention settings for a specific table.
+L343: 
+L344: You can use either PUT or PATCH, with the following difference:
+L345:   * The PUT API sets `retentionInDays` and `totalRetentionInDays` to the default value if you don't set non-null values.
+L346:   * The PATCH API doesn't change the `retentionInDays` or `totalRetentionInDays` values if you don't specify values.
+L347: 
+L348:     `PATCH https://management.azure.com/subscriptions/{subscriptionId}/resourceGroups/{resourceGroupName}/providers/Microsoft.OperationalInsights/workspaces/{workspaceName}/tables/{tableName}?api-version={apiVersion}
+L349:     Authorization: Bearer {accessToken}
+L350:     Content-Type: application/json
+L351: 
+L352:     {
+L353:       "properties": {
+L354:         "retentionInDays": 30,
+L355:         "totalRetentionInDays": 730
+L356:       }
+L357:     }
+L358:     `
+L359: Request body properties:
+L360: 
+L361: The request body includes the values in the following table.
+L362: Name  | Type  | Description
+L363: --- | --- | ---
+L364: `properties.retentionInDays`  | integer  | Analytics retention, from 4 to 730 days. Set to `-1` to inherit workspace retention. This property is read-only for Basic and Auxiliary tables. Omit it from a PATCH request to leave it unchanged.
+L365: `properties.totalRetentionInDays`  | integer  | Total retention, including long-term retention. The REST API range is 4 to 4,383 days. Set to `-1` to use `retentionInDays`, with no separate long-term retention period. Omit it from a PATCH request to leave it unchanged.
+L366: Example response:
+L367: 
+L368: Status code: 200
+L369: 
+L370:     `{
+L371:       "properties": {
+L372:         "retentionInDays": 30,
+L373:         "totalRetentionInDays": 730,
+L374:         "archiveRetentionInDays": 700
+L375:       }
+L376:     }
+L377:     `
+L378: 
+L379: Note
+L380: 
+L381: Bicep deployments are create-or-update operations, not partial PATCH operations. Preserve the existing table's schema, plan, and other settings. Use the REST tab for a retention-only PATCH.
+L382: 
+L383: The following Bicep example uses the cite32†`Microsoft.OperationalInsights/workspaces/tables` resource type.
+L384: This template includes the table resource declaration and its retention settings. Before using it to update an existing table:
+L385:   * Set `workspaceName` and `tableName` to the existing workspace and table names.
+L386:   * For an Analytics table, edit only `properties.retentionInDays` and `properties.totalRetentionInDays` as shown.
+L387:   * For Basic and Auxiliary tables, remove `properties.retentionInDays` and its parameter from this example. Set only `properties.totalRetentionInDays`; `retentionInDays` is read-only for these plans.
+L388:   * Retain the table's schema, including all column-array entries, plan, and every other setting from your maintained definition. Don't replace the existing `properties` object with only the retention members.
+L389:   * Don't deploy this example unchanged over an existing table. It doesn't include your table's complete configuration.
+L390:   * Don't update the existing workspace.
+L391:   * Keep the parent workspace as an `existing` reference. Don't mark the table you're updating as `existing`.
+L392:     `param workspaceName string = '<WorkspaceName>'
+L393:     param tableName string = '<TableName>'
