+"Jobs in Azure Container Apps | Microsoft Learn (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/container-apps/jobs)\nciteturn56view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/container-apps/jobs\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 597\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content cite1†Skip to Ask Learn chat experience L1: \nL2: This browser is no longer supported.\nL3: \nL4: Upgrade to Microsoft Edge to take advantage of the latest features, security updates, and technical support.\nL5: \nL6: cite2†Download Microsoft Edge†go.microsoft.com cite3†More info about Internet Explorer and Microsoft Edge L7: \nL8: Table of contents Exit editor mode\nL9: \nL10: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL11: \nL12: Reading mode Table of contents cite1†Read in English Add Add to Plans cite4†Edit†github.com L13: \nL14: * * *\nL15: \nL16: Copy Markdown Print\nL17: \nL18: * * *\nL19: \nL20: Note\nL21: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try cite1†signing in or changing directories.\nL22: \nL23: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try changing directories.\nL24: # Jobs in Azure Container Apps\nL25: \nL26: Feedback\nL27: \nL28: Summarize this article for me\nL29: \nL30: ## In this article\nL31: \nL32: Azure Container Apps jobs enable you to run containerized tasks that run for a finite duration and then stop. You can use jobs to perform tasks such as data processing, machine learning, or any scenario where on-demand processing is required.\nL33: \nL34: Container apps and jobs run in the same cite5†environment , allowing them to share capabilities such as networking and logging.\nL35: ## Compare container apps and jobs\nL36: \nL37: There are two types of compute resources in Azure Container Apps: apps and jobs.\nL38: \nL39: Apps are services that run continuously. If a container in an app fails, it restarts automatically. Examples of apps include HTTP APIs, web apps, and background services that continuously process input.\nL40: Jobs are tasks that start, run for a finite duration, and stop when finished. Each execution of a job typically performs a single unit of work. Job executions start manually, on a schedule, or in response to events. Examples of jobs include batch processes that run on demand and scheduled tasks.\nL41: ### Example scenarios\nL42: \nL43: The following table compares common scenarios for apps and jobs:\nL44: Container  | Compute resource  | Notes\nL45: --- | --- | ---\nL46: An HTTP server that serves web content and API requests  | App  | Configure an cite6†HTTP scale rule .\nL47: A process that generates financial reports nightly  | Job  | Use the cite7†Schedule job type and configure a cron expression.\nL48: A continuously running service that processes messages from an Azure Service Bus queue  | App  | Configure a cite8†custom scale rule .\nL49: A job that processes a single message or a small batch of messages from an Azure queue and then stops  | Job  | Use the Event job type and cite9†configure a custom scale rule to trigger job executions when there are messages in the queue.\nL50: A background task that's triggered on-demand and stops when finished  | Job  | Use the Manual job type and cite10†start executions manually or programmatically by using an API.\nL51: A self-hosted GitHub Actions runner or Azure Pipelines agent  | Job  | Use the Event job type and configure a cite11†GitHub Actions or cite12†Azure Pipelines scale rule.\nL52: An Azure Functions app  | App  | cite13†Deploy Azure Functions to Container Apps .\nL53: An event-driven app that uses the Azure WebJobs SDK  | App  | cite8†Configure a scale rule for each event source.\nL54: ## Concepts\nL55: \nL56: A Container Apps environment is a secure boundary around one or more container apps and jobs. Following are some key concepts:\nL57:   * Job: A job defines the default configuration that's used for each job execution. The configuration includes the container image to use, the resources to allocate, and the command to run.\nL58:   * Job execution: A job execution is a single run of a job that's triggered manually, on a schedule, or in response to an event.\nL59:   * Job replica: A typical job execution runs one replica defined by the job's configuration. In advanced scenarios, a job execution can run multiple replicas.\nL60: cite14†Image: Diagram that provides an overview of Container Apps jobs. L61: ## Permissions\nL62: \nL63: To start a container app job, your user account or service principal needs the appropriate permissions. Consider the following roles:\nL64: \nL65:   * Container Apps Jobs Contributor: Allows permissions to create and manage jobs.\nL66:   * Container Apps Jobs Operator: Allows permissions to read, start, and stop jobs.\nL67:   * Monitoring Reader (optional): Enables viewing monitoring data for jobs.\nL68: Both the Container Apps Jobs Contributor and Container Apps Jobs Operator roles include the `Microsoft.App/jobs/*/action` wildcard permission. This wildcard matches `Microsoft.App/jobs/listSecrets/action`, so both roles grant permission to read the job's secret values in plain text.\nL69: If neither role matches the access you want to grant, create a cite15†custom role that lists only the actions you need. For example, the following actions let a user view, start, and stop jobs without using the `Microsoft.App/jobs/*/action` wildcard. Since this rule set includes `Microsoft.App/jobs/start/action`, grant it only to identities you trust to use the job's secrets and managed identities configured to be available to its containers:\nL70:   * `Microsoft.App/jobs/read`\nL71:   * `Microsoft.App/jobs/start/action`\nL72:   * `Microsoft.App/jobs/stop/action`\nL73:   * `Microsoft.App/jobs/stop/execution/action`\nL74:   * `Microsoft.App/jobs/executions/read`\nL75:   * `Microsoft.App/jobs/execution/read`\nL76:   * `Microsoft.App/managedEnvironments/read`\nL77: \nL78: Avoid wildcard patterns such as `Microsoft.App/jobs/*/action` in a custom role definition. This wildcard grants every current and future operation that matches the pattern, including `listSecrets`.\nL79: \nL80: Warning\nL81: Permission to start a job can provide access to that job's secrets even when the role doesn't include the `listSecrets` action. The cite16†Jobs - Start REST API accepts an optional execution template that can override the main and init container images, commands, and environment variables. A user who holds `Microsoft.App/jobs/start/action` and knows a secret's name can reference that secret from a container of their choosing and read its value inside the container.\nL82: The container can also use any managed identity cite17†configured to be available to it . Grant permission to start a job only to identities you trust to use the job's secrets and available managed identities.\nL83: For more information, see cite18†Permissions for managing secrets and cite19†Azure role-based access control .\nL84: ## Job trigger types\nL85: \nL86: A job's trigger type determines how the job is started. The following trigger types are available:\nL87: \nL88:   * Manual: Manual jobs are triggered on demand.\nL89:   * Schedule: Scheduled jobs are triggered at specific times and can run repeatedly.\nL90:   * Event: Event-driven jobs are triggered by events, such as a message arriving in a queue.\nL91: ### Manual jobs\nL92: \nL93: Manual jobs are triggered on demand via the Azure CLI, the Azure portal, or a request to the Azure Resource Manager API.\nL94: \nL95: Examples of manual jobs include:\nL96: \nL97:   * A one-time processing task like migrating data from one system to another.\nL98:   * An e-commerce site running as a container app starts a job execution to process inventory when an order is placed.\nL99: \nL100: To create a manual job, use the job type `Manual`.\nL101: \nL102:   * cite20†Azure CLI L103:   * cite21†Azure Resource Manager L104:   * cite22†Azure portal L105: To create a manual job by using the Azure CLI, use the `az containerapp job create` command. The following example creates a manual job named `my-job` in a resource group named `my-resource-group` and a Container Apps environment named `my-environment`:\nL106: \nL107:     `az containerapp job create \\\nL108:         --name \"my-job\" --resource-group \"my-resource-group\"  --environment \"my-environment\" \\\nL109:         --trigger-type \"Manual\" \\\nL110:         --replica-timeout 1800 \\\nL111:         --replica-retry-limit 0 \\\nL112:         --replica-completion-count 1 \\\nL113:         --parallelism 1 \\\nL114:         --image \"mcr.microsoft.com/k8se/quickstart-jobs:latest\" \\\nL115:         --cpu \"0.25\" --memory \"0.5Gi\"\nL116:     `\nL117: The following partial example of an Azure Resource Manager template creates a manual job named `my-job` in a resource group named `my-resource-group` and a Container Apps environment named `my-environment`:\nL118: \nL119:     `{\nL120:         \"location\": \"North Central US\",\nL121:         \"name\": \"my-job\",\nL122:         \"properties\": {\nL123:             \"configuration\": {\nL124:                 \"manualTriggerConfig\": {\nL125:                     \"parallelism\": 1,\nL126:                     \"replicaCompletionCount\": 1\nL127:                 },\nL128:                 \"replicaRetryLimit\": 0,\nL129:                 \"replicaTimeout\": 1800,\nL130:                 \"triggerType\": \"Manual\"\nL131:             },\nL132:             \"environmentId\": \"/subscriptions/<subscription_id>/resourceGroups/my-resource-group/providers/Microsoft.App/managedEnvironments/my-environment\",\nL133:             \"template\": {\nL134:                 \"containers\": [\nL135:                     {\nL136:                         \"image\": \"mcr.microsoft.com/k8se/quickstart-jobs:latest\",\nL137:                         \"name\": \"main\",\nL138:                         \"resources\": {\nL139:                             \"cpu\": 0.25,\nL140:                             \"memory\": \"0.5Gi\"\nL141:                         }\nL142:                     }\nL143:                 ]\nL144:             }\nL145:         }\nL146:     }\nL147:     `\nL148: To create a manual job by using the Azure portal, search for Container App Jobs in the Azure portal and select Create. Specify Manual as the trigger type.\nL149: \nL150: To use a sample container image, enter the following values on the Container tab:\nL151: \nL152: Setting  | Value\nL153: --- | ---\nL154: Name  | main\nL155: Image source  | Docker Hub or other registries\nL156: Image type  | Public\nL157: Registry login server  | mcr.microsoft.com\nL158: Image and tag  | k8se/quickstart-jobs:latest\nL159: CPU and memory  | 0.25 CPU cores, 0.5 Gi memory, or higher\nL160: The `mcr.microsoft.com/k8se/quickstart-jobs:latest` image is a public sample container image that runs a job that waits a few seconds, prints a message to the console, and then stops. For information about authenticating and using a private container image, see cite23†Containers .\nL161: \nL162: The preceding command only creates the job. To start a job execution, see cite10†Start a job execution on demand .\nL163: ### Scheduled jobs\nL164: \nL165: To create a scheduled job, use the job type `Schedule`.\nL166: \nL167: Container Apps jobs use cron expressions to define schedules. They support the standard cite24†cron†en.wikipedia.org expression format with five fields for minute, hour, day of the month, month, and day of the week. Here are some examples of cron expressions:\nL168: Expression  | Description\nL169: --- | ---\nL170: `*/5 * * * *`  | Runs every 5 minutes.\nL171: `0 */2 * * *`  | Runs every two hours.\nL172: `0 0 * * *`  | Runs every day at midnight.\nL173: `0 0 * * 0`  | Runs every Sunday at midnight.\nL174: `0 0 1 * *`  | Runs on the first day of every month at midnight.\nL175: \nL176: Cron expressions in scheduled jobs are evaluated in Coordinated Universal Time (UTC).\nL177: \nL178:   * cite25†Azure CLI L179:   * cite26†Azure Resource Manager L180:   * cite27†Azure portal L181: To create a scheduled job by using the Azure CLI, use the `az containerapp job create` command. The following example creates a scheduled job named `my-job` in a resource group named `my-resource-group` and a Container Apps environment named `my-environment`:\nL182: \nL183:     `az containerapp job create \\\nL184:         --name \"my-job\" --resource-group \"my-resource-group\"  --environment \"my-environment\" \\\nL185:         --trigger-type \"Schedule\" \\\nL186:         --replica-timeout 1800 \\\nL187:         --replica-retry-limit 0 \\\nL188:         --parallelism 1 \\\nL189:         --replica-completion-count 1 \\\nL190:         --image \"mcr.microsoft.com/k8se/quickstart-jobs:latest\" \\\nL191:         --cpu \"0.25\" --memory \"0.5Gi\" \\\nL192:         --cron-expression \"*/1 * * * *\"\nL193:     `\nL194: The following partial example of an Azure Resource Manager template creates a manual job named `my-job` in a resource group named `my-resource-group` and a Container Apps environment named `my-environment`:\nL195: \nL196:     `{\nL197:         \"location\": \"North Central US\",\nL198:         \"name\": \"my-job\",\nL199:         \"properties\": {\nL200:             \"configuration\": {\nL201:                 \"scheduleTriggerConfig\": {\nL202:                     \"cronExpression\": \"*/1 * * * *\",\nL203:                     \"parallelism\": 1,\nL204:                     \"replicaCompletionCount\": 1\nL205:                 },\nL206:                 \"replicaRetryLimit\": 0,\nL207:                 \"replicaTimeout\": 1800,\nL208:                 \"triggerType\": \"Schedule\"\nL209:             },\nL210:             \"environmentId\": \"/subscriptions/<subscription_id>/resourceGroups/my-resource-group/providers/Microsoft.App/managedEnvironments/my-environment\",\nL211:             \"template\": {\nL212:                 \"containers\": [\nL213:                     {\nL214:                         \"image\": \"mcr.microsoft.com/k8se/quickstart-jobs:latest\",\nL215:                         \"name\": \"main\",\nL216:                         \"resources\": {\nL217:                             \"cpu\": 0.25,\nL218:                             \"memory\": \"0.5Gi\"\nL219:                         }\nL220:                     }\nL221:                 ]\nL222:             }\nL223:         }\nL224:     }\nL225:     `\nL226: To create a scheduled job by using the Azure portal, search for Container App Jobs in the Azure portal and select Create. Specify Schedule as the trigger type and define the schedule with a cron expression, such as `*/1 * * * *` to run every minute.\nL227: \nL228: To use a sample container image, enter the following values on the Container tab:\nL229: Setting  | Value\nL230: --- | ---\nL231: Name  | main\nL232: Image source  | Docker Hub or other registries\nL233: Image type  | Public\nL234: Registry login server  | mcr.microsoft.com\nL235: Image and tag  | k8se/quickstart-jobs:latest\nL236: CPU and memory  | 0.25 CPU cores, 0.5 Gi memory, or higher\nL237: The `mcr.microsoft.com/k8se/quickstart-jobs:latest` image is a public sample container image that runs a job that waits a few seconds, prints a message to the console, and then stops. For information about authenticating and using a private container image, see cite23†Containers .\nL238: \nL239: The cron expression `*/1 * * * *` runs the job every minute.\nL240: ### Event-driven jobs\nL241: \nL242: Events from supported cite8†custom scalers trigger event-driven jobs. Examples of event-driven jobs include:\nL243: \nL244:   * A job that runs when a new message is added to a queue like Azure Service Bus, Kafka, or RabbitMQ.\nL245:   * A self-hosted cite11†GitHub Actions runner or cite12†Azure DevOps agent that runs when a new job is queued in a workflow or pipeline.\nL246: Container apps and event-driven jobs use cite28†KEDA†keda.sh scalers. They both evaluate scaling rules on a polling interval to measure the volume of events for an event source, but the way they use the results is different.\nL247: \nL248: In an app, each replica continuously processes events, and a scaling rule determines the number of replicas to run to meet demand. In event-driven jobs, each job execution typically processes a single event, and a scaling rule determines the number of job executions to run.\n"

