+"Jobs in Azure Container Apps | Microsoft Learn (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/container-apps/jobs)\nciteturn57view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/container-apps/jobs\",\"pattern\":\"storage\"}); Total lines: 597\nL44: Container  | Compute resource  | Notes\nL45: --- | --- | ---\nL46: An HTTP server that serves web content and API requests  | App  | Configure an cite6†HTTP scale rule .\nL49: A job that processes a single message or a small batch of messages from an Azure queue and then stops  | Job  | Use the Event job type and cite9†configure a custom scale rule to trigger job executions when there are messages in the queue.\nL50: A background task that's triggered on-demand and stops when finished  | Job  | Use the Manual job type and cite10†start executions manually or programmatically by using an API.\nL51: A self-hosted GitHub Actions runner or Azure Pipelines agent  | Job  | Use the Event job type and configure a cite11†GitHub Actions or cite12†Azure Pipelines scale rule.\nL52: An Azure Functions app  | App  | cite13†Deploy Azure Functions to Container Apps .\nL53: An event-driven app that uses the Azure WebJobs SDK  | App  | cite8†Configure a scale rule for each event source.\nL54: ## Concepts\nL55: \nL56: A Container Apps environment is a secure boundary around one or more container apps and jobs. Following are some key concepts:\nL57:   * Job: A job defines the default configuration that's used for each job execution. The configuration includes the container image to use, the resources to allocate, and the command to run.\nL58:   * Job execution: A job execution is a single run of a job that's triggered manually, on a schedule, or in response to an event.\nL59:   * Job replica: A typical job execution runs one replica defined by the job's configuration. In advanced scenarios, a job execution can run multiple replicas.\nL60: cite14†Image: Diagram that provides an overview of Container Apps jobs. L61: ## Permissions\nL62: \nL63: To start a container app job, your user account or service principal needs the appropriate permissions. Consider the following roles:\nL64: \nL65:   * Container Apps Jobs Contributor: Allows permissions to create and manage jobs.\nL66:   * Container Apps Jobs Operator: Allows permissions to read, start, and stop jobs.\nL67:   * Monitoring Reader (optional): Enables viewing monitoring data for jobs.\nL68: Both the Container Apps Jobs Contributor and Container Apps Jobs Operator roles include the `Microsoft.App/jobs/*/action` wildcard permission. This wildcard matches `Microsoft.App/jobs/listSecrets/action`, so both roles grant permission to read the job's secret values in plain text.\nL69: If neither role matches the access you want to grant, create a cite15†custom role that lists only the actions you need. For example, the following actions let a user view, start, and stop jobs without using the `Microsoft.App/jobs/*/action` wildcard. Since this rule set includes `Microsoft.App/jobs/start/action`, grant it only to identities you trust to use the job's secrets and managed identities configured to be available to its containers:\nL70:   * `Microsoft.App/jobs/read`\nL71:   * `Microsoft.App/jobs/start/action`\nL72:   * `Microsoft.App/jobs/stop/action`\nL73:   * `Microsoft.App/jobs/stop/execution/action`\nL74:   * `Microsoft.App/jobs/executions/read`\nL75:   * `Microsoft.App/jobs/execution/read`\nL76:   * `Microsoft.App/managedEnvironments/read`\nL77: \nL78: Avoid wildcard patterns such as `Microsoft.App/jobs/*/action` in a custom role definition. This wildcard grants every current and future operation that matches the pattern, including `listSecrets`.\nL79: \nL80: Warning\nL81: Permission to start a job can provide access to that job's secrets even when the role doesn't include the `listSecrets` action. The cite16†Jobs - Start REST API accepts an optional execution template that can override the main and init container images, commands, and environment variables. A user who holds `Microsoft.App/jobs/start/action` and knows a secret's name can reference that secret from a container of their choosing and read its value inside the container.\nL82: The container can also use any managed identity cite17†configured to be available to it . Grant permission to start a job only to identities you trust to use the job's secrets and available managed identities.\nL83: For more information, see cite18†Permissions for managing secrets and cite19†Azure role-based access control .\nL84: ## Job trigger types\nL85: \nL86: A job's trigger type determines how the job is started. The following trigger types are available:\nL87: \nL88:   * Manual: Manual jobs are triggered on demand.\nL89:   * Schedule: Scheduled jobs are triggered at specific times and can run repeatedly.\nL90:   * Event: Event-driven jobs are triggered by events, such as a message arriving in a queue.\nL91: ### Manual jobs\nL92: \nL93: Manual jobs are triggered on demand via the Azure CLI, the Azure portal, or a request to the Azure Resource Manager API.\nL94: \nL95: Examples of manual jobs include:\nL96: \nL97:   * A one-time processing task like migrating data from one system to another.\nL98:   * An e-commerce site running as a container app starts a job execution to process inventory when an order is placed.\nL99: \nL100: To create a manual job, use the job type `Manual`.\nL101: \nL102:   * cite20†Azure CLI L103:   * cite21†Azure Resource Manager L104:   * cite22†Azure portal L105: To create a manual job by using the Azure CLI, use the `az containerapp job create` command. The following example creates a manual job named `my-job` in a resource group named `my-resource-group` and a Container Apps environment named `my-environment`:\nL106: \nL107:     `az containerapp job create \\\nL108:         --name \"my-job\" --resource-group \"my-resource-group\"  --environment \"my-environment\" \\\nL109:         --trigger-type \"Manual\" \\\nL110:         --replica-timeout 1800 \\\nL111:         --replica-retry-limit 0 \\\nL112:         --replica-completion-count 1 \\\nL113:         --parallelism 1 \\\nL114:         --image \"mcr.microsoft.com/k8se/quickstart-jobs:latest\" \\\nL115:         --cpu \"0.25\" --memory \"0.5Gi\"\nL116:     `\nL117: The following partial example of an Azure Resource Manager template creates a manual job named `my-job` in a resource group named `my-resource-group` and a Container Apps environment named `my-environment`:\nL118: \nL119:     `{\nL120:         \"location\": \"North Central US\",\nL121:         \"name\": \"my-job\",\nL122:         \"properties\": {\nL123:             \"configuration\": {\nL124:                 \"manualTriggerConfig\": {\nL125:                     \"parallelism\": 1,\nL126:                     \"replicaCompletionCount\": 1\nL127:                 },\nL128:                 \"replicaRetryLimit\": 0,\nL129:                 \"replicaTimeout\": 1800,\nL130:                 \"triggerType\": \"Manual\"\nL131:             },\nL132:             \"environmentId\": \"/subscriptions/<subscription_id>/resourceGroups/my-resource-group/providers/Microsoft.App/managedEnvironments/my-environment\",\nL133:             \"template\": {\nL134:                 \"containers\": [\nL135:                     {\nL136:                         \"image\": \"mcr.microsoft.com/k8se/quickstart-jobs:latest\",\nL137:                         \"name\": \"main\",\nL138:                         \"resources\": {\nL139:                             \"cpu\": 0.25,\nL140:                             \"memory\": \"0.5Gi\"\nL141:                         }\nL142:                     }\nL143:                 ]\nL144:             }\nL145:         }\nL146:     }\nL147:     `\nL148: To create a manual job by using the Azure portal, search for Container App Jobs in the Azure portal and select Create. Specify Manual as the trigger type.\nL149: \nL150: To use a sample container image, enter the following values on the Container tab:\nL151: \nL152: Setting  | Value\nL153: --- | ---\nL154: Name  | main\nL155: Image source  | Docker Hub or other registries\nL156: Image type  | Public\nL157: Registry login server  | mcr.microsoft.com\nL158: Image and tag  | k8se/quickstart-jobs:latest\nL159: CPU and memory  | 0.25 CPU cores, 0.5 Gi memory, or higher\nL160: The `mcr.microsoft.com/k8se/quickstart-jobs:latest` image is a public sample container image that runs a job that waits a few seconds, prints a message to the console, and then stops. For information about authenticating and using a private container image, see cite23†Containers .\nL161: \nL162: The preceding command only creates the job. To start a job execution, see cite10†Start a job execution on demand .\nL163: ### Scheduled jobs\nL164: \nL165: To create a scheduled job, use the job type `Schedule`.\nL166: \nL167: Container Apps jobs use cron expressions to define schedules. They support the standard cite24†cron†en.wikipedia.org expression format with five fields for minute, hour, day of the month, month, and day of the week. Here are some examples of cron expressions:\nL168: Expression  | Description\nL169: --- | ---\nL170: `*/5 * * * *`  | Runs every 5 minutes.\nL171: `0 */2 * * *`  | Runs every two hours.\nL172: `0 0 * * *`  | Runs every day at midnight.\nL173: `0 0 * * 0`  | Runs every Sunday at midnight.\nL174: `0 0 1 * *`  | Runs on the first day of every month at midnight.\nL175: \nL176: Cron expressions in scheduled jobs are evaluated in Coordinated Universal Time (UTC).\nL177: \nL178:   * cite25†Azure CLI L179:   * cite26†Azure Resource Manager L180:   * cite27†Azure portal L181: To create a scheduled job by using the Azure CLI, use the `az containerapp job create` command. The following example creates a scheduled job named `my-job` in a resource group named `my-resource-group` and a Container Apps environment named `my-environment`:\nL182: \nL183:     `az containerapp job create \\\nL184:         --name \"my-job\" --resource-group \"my-resource-group\"  --environment \"my-environment\" \\\nL185:         --trigger-type \"Schedule\" \\\nL186:         --replica-timeout 1800 \\\nL187:         --replica-retry-limit 0 \\\nL188:         --parallelism 1 \\\nL189:         --replica-completion-count 1 \\\nL190:         --image \"mcr.microsoft.com/k8se/quickstart-jobs:latest\" \\\nL191:         --cpu \"0.25\" --memory \"0.5Gi\" \\\nL192:         --cron-expression \"*/1 * * * *\"\nL193:     `\nL194: The following partial example of an Azure Resource Manager template creates a manual job named `my-job` in a resource group named `my-resource-group` and a Container Apps environment named `my-environment`:\nL195: \nL196:     `{\nL197:         \"location\": \"North Central US\",\nL198:         \"name\": \"my-job\",\nL199:         \"properties\": {\nL200:             \"configuration\": {\nL201:                 \"scheduleTriggerConfig\": {\nL202:                     \"cronExpression\": \"*/1 * * * *\",\nL203:                     \"parallelism\": 1,\nL204:                     \"replicaCompletionCount\": 1\nL205:                 },\nL206:                 \"replicaRetryLimit\": 0,\nL207:                 \"replicaTimeout\": 1800,\nL208:                 \"triggerType\": \"Schedule\"\nL209:             },\nL210:             \"environmentId\": \"/subscriptions/<subscription_id>/resourceGroups/my-resource-group/providers/Microsoft.App/managedEnvironments/my-environment\",\nL211:             \"template\": {\nL212:                 \"containers\": [\nL213:                     {\nL214:                         \"image\": \"mcr.microsoft.com/k8se/quickstart-jobs:latest\",\nL215:                         \"name\": \"main\",\nL216:                         \"resources\": {\nL217:                             \"cpu\": 0.25,\nL218:                             \"memory\": \"0.5Gi\"\nL219:                         }\nL220:                     }\nL221:                 ]\nL222:             }\nL223:         }\nL224:     }\nL225:     `\nL226: To create a scheduled job by using the Azure portal, search for Container App Jobs in the Azure portal and select Create. Specify Schedule as the trigger type and define the schedule with a cron expression, such as `*/1 * * * *` to run every minute.\nL227: \nL228: To use a sample container image, enter the following values on the Container tab:\nL229: Setting  | Value\nL230: --- | ---\nL231: Name  | main\nL232: Image source  | Docker Hub or other registries\nL233: Image type  | Public\nL234: Registry login server  | mcr.microsoft.com\nL235: Image and tag  | k8se/quickstart-jobs:latest\nL236: CPU and memory  | 0.25 CPU cores, 0.5 Gi memory, or higher\nL237: The `mcr.microsoft.com/k8se/quickstart-jobs:latest` image is a public sample container image that runs a job that waits a few seconds, prints a message to the console, and then stops. For information about authenticating and using a private container image, see cite23†Containers .\nL238: \nL239: The cron expression `*/1 * * * *` runs the job every minute.\nL240: ### Event-driven jobs\nL241: \nL242: Events from supported cite8†custom scalers trigger event-driven jobs. Examples of event-driven jobs include:\nL243: \nL244:   * A job that runs when a new message is added to a queue like Azure Service Bus, Kafka, or RabbitMQ.\nL245:   * A self-hosted cite11†GitHub Actions runner or cite12†Azure DevOps agent that runs when a new job is queued in a workflow or pipeline.\nL246: Container apps and event-driven jobs use cite28†KEDA†keda.sh scalers. They both evaluate scaling rules on a polling interval to measure the volume of events for an event source, but the way they use the results is different.\nL247: \nL248: In an app, each replica continuously processes events, and a scaling rule determines the number of replicas to run to meet demand. In event-driven jobs, each job execution typically processes a single event, and a scaling rule determines the number of job executions to run.\nL249: Use jobs when each event requires a new instance of the container with dedicated resources or needs to run for a long time. Event-driven jobs are conceptually similar to cite29†KEDA scaling jobs†keda.sh .\nL250: \nL251: To create an event-driven job, use the job type `Event`.\nL252: \nL253:   * cite30†Azure CLI L254:   * cite31†Azure Resource Manager L255:   * cite32†Azure portal L256: To create an event-driven job by using the Azure CLI, use the `az containerapp job create` command. The following example creates an event-driven job named `my-job` in a resource group named `my-resource-group` and a Container Apps environment named `my-environment`:\nL257: \nL258:     `az containerapp job create \\\nL259:         --name \"my-job\" --resource-group \"my-resource-group\"  --environment \"my-environment\" \\\nL260:         --trigger-type \"Event\" \\\nL261:         --replica-timeout 1800 \\\nL262:         --image \"docker.io/myuser/my-event-driven-job:latest\" \\\nL263:         --cpu \"0.25\" --memory \"0.5Gi\" \\\nL264:         --min-executions \"0\" \\\nL265:         --max-executions \"10\" \\\nL266:         --scale-rule-name \"queue\" \\\nL267:         --scale-rule-type \"azure-queue\" \\\nL268:         --scale-rule-metadata \"accountName=mystorage\" \"queueName=myqueue\" \"queueLength=1\" \\\nL269:         --scale-rule-auth \"connection=connection-string-secret\" \\\nL270:         --secrets \"connection-string-secret=<QUEUE_CONNECTION_STRING>\"\nL271:     `\nL272: The example configures an Azure Storage queue scale rule.\nL273: The following partial example of an Azure Resource Manager template creates an event-driven job named `my-job` in a resource group named `my-resource-group` and a Container Apps environment named `my-environment`:\nL274: \nL275:     `{\nL276:         \"location\": \"North Central US\",\nL277:         \"name\": \"my-job\",\nL278:         \"properties\": {\nL279:             \"configuration\": {\nL280:                 \"eventTriggerConfig\": {\nL281:                     \"scale\": {\nL282:                         \"maxExecutions\": 10,\nL283:                         \"minExecutions\": 0,\nL284:                         \"pollingInterval\": 15,\nL285:                         \"rules\": [\nL286:                             {\nL287:                                 \"auth\": [\nL288:                                     {\nL289:                                         \"triggerParameter\": \"connection\",\nL290:                                         \"secretRef\": \"connection-string-secret\"\nL291:                                     }\nL292:                                 ],\nL293:                                 \"metadata\": {\nL294:                                     \"accountName\": \"mystorage\",\nL295:                                     \"queueLength\": 1,\nL296:                                     \"queueName\": \"myqueue\"\nL297:                                 },\nL298:                                 \"name\": \"queue\",\nL299:                                 \"type\": \"azure-queue\"\nL300:                             }\nL301:                         ]\nL302:                     }\nL303:                 },\nL304:                 \"replicaRetryLimit\": 0,\nL305:                 \"replicaTimeout\": 1800,\nL306:                 \"triggerType\": \"Event\",\nL307:                 \"secrets\": [\nL308:                     {\nL309:                         \"name\": \"connection-string-secret\",\nL310:                         \"value\": \"<QUEUE_CONNECTION_STRING>\"\nL311:                     }\nL312:                 ]\nL313:             },\nL314:             \"environmentId\": \"/subscriptions/<SUBSCRIPTION_ID>/resourceGroups/my-resource-group/providers/Microsoft.App/managedEnvironments/my-environment\",\nL315:             \"template\": {\nL316:                 \"containers\": [\nL317:                     {\nL318:                         \"image\": \"docker.io/myuser/my-event-driven-job:latest\",\nL319:                         \"name\": \"main\",\nL320:                         \"resources\": {\nL321:                             \"cpu\": 0.25,\nL322:                             \"memory\": \"0.5Gi\"\nL323:                         }\nL324:                     }\nL325:                 ]\nL326:             }\nL327:         }\nL328:     }\nL329:     `\nL330: The example configures an Azure Storage queue scale rule.\nL331: \nL332: To create an event-driven job by using the Azure portal, search for Container App Jobs in the Azure portal and select Create. Specify Event-driven as the trigger type and configure the scaling rule.\nL333: \nL334: For a complete tutorial, see cite9†Deploy an event-driven job .\nL335: ## Start a job execution on demand\nL336: \nL337: For any job type, you can start a job execution on demand.\nL338: \nL339:   * cite33†Azure CLI L340:   * cite34†Azure Resource Manager L341:   * cite35†Azure portal L342: \nL343: To start a job execution by using the Azure CLI, use the `az containerapp job start` command. The following example starts an execution of a job named `my-job` in a resource group named `my-resource-group`:\nL344: \nL345:     `az containerapp job start --name \"my-job\" --resource-group \"my-resource-group\"\nL346:     `\nL347: To start a job execution by using the Azure Resource Manager REST API, make a `POST` request to the job's `start` operation.\nL348: \nL349: The following example starts an execution of a job named `my-job` in a resource group named `my-resource-group`:\nL350: \nL351:     `POST https://management.azure.com/subscriptions/<SUBSCRIPTION_ID>/resourceGroups/my-resource-group/providers/Microsoft.App/jobs/my-job/start?api-version=2023-05-01\nL352:     Authorization: Bearer <TOKEN>\nL353:     `\nL354: \nL355: Replace `<SUBSCRIPTION_ID>` with your subscription ID.\nL356: To authenticate the request, replace `<TOKEN>` in the `Authorization` header with a valid bearer token. The identity used to generate the token must have the `Microsoft.App/jobs/start/action` permission on the Container Apps job resource. The Container Apps Jobs Operator and Container Apps Jobs Contributor built-in roles include this permission. For more information, see the cite16†Jobs - Start REST API .\nL357: \nL358: To start a job execution in the Azure portal, select Run now on the job's overview page.\nL359: When you start a job execution, you can choose to override the job's configuration. For example, you can override an environment variable or the startup command to run the same job with different inputs. The overridden configuration is only used for the current execution and doesn't change the job's configuration.\nL360: \nL361: When you override a configuration, the job's entire template configuration is replaced with the new configuration. Ensure that the new configuration includes all required settings.\nL362: \nL363: Warning\nL364: An identity that can start a job can use an execution template to reference job secrets whose names it knows and use managed identities configured to be available to the container. For more information, see cite36†Permissions .\nL365: \nL366:   * cite37†Azure CLI L367:   * cite38†Azure Resource Manager L368:   * cite39†Azure portal L369: To override the job's configuration when you start an execution, use the `az containerapp job start` command and pass a YAML file containing the template to use for the execution. The following example starts an execution of a job named `my-job` in a resource group named `my-resource-group`.\nL370: Retrieve the job's current configuration with the `az containerapp job show` command, and save the template to a file named `my-job-template.yaml`:\nL371: \nL372:     `az containerapp job show --name \"my-job\" --resource-group \"my-resource-group\" --query \"properties.template\" --output yaml > my-job-template.yaml\nL373:     `\nL374: \nL375: The `--query \"properties.template\"` option returns only the job's template configuration.\nL376: Edit the `my-job-template.yaml` file to override the job's configuration. For example, to override the environment variables, modify the `env` section:\nL377: \nL378:     `containers:\nL379:     - name: print-hello\nL380:       image: ubuntu\nL381:       resources:\nL382:         cpu: 1\nL383:         memory: 2Gi\nL384:       env:\nL385:       - name: MY_NAME\nL386:         value: Azure Container Apps jobs\nL387:       args:\nL388:       - /bin/bash\nL389:       - -c\nL390:       - echo \"Hello, $MY_NAME!\"\nL391:     `\nL392: Start the job using the template:\nL393: \nL394:     `az containerapp job start --name \"my-job\" --resource-group \"my-resource-group\" \\\nL395:         --yaml my-job-template.yaml\nL396:     `\nL397: To override the job's configuration, include a template in the request body. The following example overrides the startup command to run a different command:\nL398: \nL399:     `POST https://management.azure.com/subscriptions/<SUBSCRIPTION_ID>/resourceGroups/my-resource-group/providers/Microsoft.App/jobs/my-job/start?api-version=2023-05-01\nL400:     Content-Type: application/json\nL401:     Authorization: Bearer <TOKEN>\nL402: \nL403:     {\nL404:         \"containers\": [\nL405:             {\nL406:                 \"image\": \"mcr.microsoft.com/k8se/quickstart-jobs:latest\",\nL407:                 \"name\": \"main\",\nL408:                 \"resources\": {\nL409:                     \"cpu\": 0.25,\nL410:                     \"memory\": \"0.5Gi\"\nL411:                 },\nL412:                 \"command\": [\nL413:                     \"echo\",\nL414:                     \"Hello, Azure Container Apps jobs!\"\nL415:                 ]\nL416:             }\nL417:         ]\nL418:     }\nL419:     `\nL420: Replace `<SUBSCRIPTION_ID>` with your subscription ID and `<TOKEN>` in the `Authorization` header with a valid bearer token. The identity used to generate the token must have the `Microsoft.App/jobs/start/action` permission on the Container Apps job resource. The Container Apps Jobs Operator and Container Apps Jobs Contributor built-in roles include this permission. For more information, see the cite16†Jobs - Start REST API .\nL421: Starting a job execution with an overridden configuration isn't supported in the Azure portal.\nL422: ## Get job execution history\nL423: \nL424: Each Container Apps job maintains a history of recent job executions.\nL425: \nL426:   * cite40†Azure CLI L427:   * cite41†Azure Resource Manager L428:   * cite42†Azure portal L429: To get the statuses of job executions by using the Azure CLI, use the `az containerapp job execution list` command. The following example returns the status of the most recent execution of a job named `my-job` in a resource group named `my-resource-group`:\nL430: \nL431:     `az containerapp job execution list --name \"my-job\" --resource-group \"my-resource-group\"\nL432:     `\nL433: To get the status of job executions by using the Azure Resource Manager REST API, make a `GET` request to the job's `executions` operation. The following example returns the status of the most recent execution of a job named `my-job` in a resource group named `my-resource-group`:\nL434: \nL435:     `GET https://management.azure.com/subscriptions/<SUBSCRIPTION_ID>/resourceGroups/my-resource-group/providers/Microsoft.App/jobs/my-job/executions?api-version=2023-05-01\nL436:     `\nL437: Replace `<SUBSCRIPTION_ID>` with your subscription ID.\nL438: \nL439: To authenticate the request, add an `Authorization` header that contains a valid bearer token. For more information, see cite43†Azure REST API reference .\nL440: \nL441: To view the status of job executions by using the Azure portal, search for Container App Jobs in the Azure portal and select the job. In the left pane, under Monitoring, select Execution history to see the status of recent executions.\nL442: The execution history for scheduled and event-based jobs is limited to the most recent 100 successful and failed job executions.\nL443: \nL444: To list all executions of a job or to get detailed output from a job, query the logs provider configured for your Container Apps environment.\nL445: ## Advanced job configuration\nL446: \nL447: Container Apps jobs support advanced configuration options such as container settings, retries, timeouts, and parallelism.\nL448: \nL449: ### Container settings\nL450: \nL451: Container settings define the containers to run in each replica of a job execution. They include environment variables, secrets, and resource limits. For more information, see cite44†Containers . Running multiple containers in a single job is an advanced scenario. Most jobs run a single container.\nL452: ### Job settings\nL453: \nL454: The following table includes the job settings that you can configure:\nL455: Setting  | Azure Resource Manager property  | CLI parameter  | Description\nL456: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL457: Job type  | `triggerType`  | `--trigger-type`  | The type of job (`Manual`, `Schedule`, or `Event`).\nL458: Replica timeout  | `replicaTimeout`  | `--replica-timeout`  | The maximum time in seconds to wait for a replica to complete.\nL459: Polling interval  | `pollingInterval`  | `--polling-interval`  | The time in seconds to wait between polling for events. The default is 30 seconds.\nL460: Replica retry limit  | `replicaRetryLimit`  | `--replica-retry-limit`  | The maximum number of times to retry a failed replica. To fail a replica without retrying, set the value to `0`. The `replicaTimeout` setting takes precedence if it expires before all retries occur.\nL461: Parallelism  | `parallelism`  | `--parallelism`  | The number of replicas to run per execution. For most jobs, set the value to `1`.\nL462: Replica completion count  | `replicaCompletionCount`  | `--replica-completion-count`  | The number of replicas to complete successfully for the execution to succeed. Most be equal to or less than the parallelism. For most jobs, set the value to `1`.\nL463: ### Long-running jobs\nL464: \nL465: Note\nL466: \nL467: Platform maintenance, including routine security updates and upgrades, might interrupt long-running job replicas. Set the replica retry limit to at least `1`, and design your workload for at-least-once processing so that retries can safely complete the work.\nL468: ### Example\nL469: \nL470:   * cite45†Azure CLI L471:   * cite46†Azure Resource Manager L472:   * cite47†Azure portal L473: The following example creates a job with advanced configuration options:\nL474: \nL475:     `az containerapp job create \\\nL476:         --name \"my-job\" --resource-group \"my-resource-group\"  --environment \"my-environment\" \\\nL477:         --trigger-type \"Schedule\" \\\nL478:         --replica-timeout 1800 --replica-retry-limit 3 --replica-completion-count 5 --parallelism 5 \\\nL479:         --image \"myregistry.azurecr.io/quickstart-jobs:latest\" \\\nL480:         --cpu \"0.25\" --memory \"0.5Gi\" \\\nL481:         --command \"/startup.sh\" \\\nL482:         --env-vars \"MY_ENV_VAR=my-value\" \\\nL483:         --cron-expression \"0 0 * * *\"  \\\nL484:         --registry-server \"myregistry.azurecr.io\" \\\nL485:         --registry-username \"myregistry\" \\\nL486:         --registry-password \"myregistrypassword\"\nL487:     `\nL488: The following partial example of an Azure Resource Manager template creates a job with advanced configuration options:\nL489: \nL490:     `{\nL491:         \"location\": \"North Central US\",\nL492:         \"name\": \"my-job\",\nL493:         \"properties\": {\nL494:             \"configuration\": {\nL495:                 \"scheduleTriggerConfig\": {\nL496:                     \"cronExpression\": \"0 0 * * *\",\nL497:                     \"parallelism\": 5,\nL498:                     \"replicaCompletionCount\": 5\nL499:                 },\nL500:                 \"replicaRetryLimit\": 3,\nL501:                 \"replicaTimeout\": 1800,\nL502:                 \"triggerType\": \"Schedule\",\nL503:                 \"secrets\": [\nL504:                     {\nL505:                         \"name\": \"registry-password\",\nL506:                         \"value\": \"myregistrypassword\"\nL507:                     }\nL508:                 ]\nL509:                 \"registries\": [\nL510:                     {\nL511:                         \"server\": \"myregistry.azurecr.io\",\nL512:                         \"username\": \"myregistry\",\nL513:                         \"passwordSecretRef\": \"registry-password\"\nL514:                     }\nL515:                 ]\nL516:             },\nL517:             \"environmentId\": \"/subscriptions/<subscription_id>/resourceGroups/my-resource-group/providers/Microsoft.App/managedEnvironments/my-environment\",\nL518:             \"template\": {\nL519:                 \"containers\": [\nL520:                     {\nL521:                         \"command\": [\nL522:                             \"/startup.sh\"\nL523:                         ],\nL524:                         \"cpu\": 0.25,\nL525:                         \"envVars\": [\nL526:                             {\nL527:                                 \"name\": \"MY_ENV_VAR\",\nL528:                                 \"value\": \"my-value\"\nL529:                             }\nL530:                         ],\nL531:                         \"image\": \"myregistry.azurecr.io/quickstart-jobs:latest\",\nL532:                         \"memory\": \"0.5Gi\",\nL533:                         \"name\": \"main\",\nL534:                         \"resources\": {\nL535:                             \"cpu\": 0.25,\nL536:                             \"memory\": \"0.5Gi\"\nL537:                         }\nL538:                     }\nL539:                 ]\nL540:             }\nL541:         }\nL542:     }\nL543:     `\nL544: To configure advanced settings by using the Azure portal, create the job as described earlier in this article and then, under Settings in the left pane, select Configuration. Edit the configuration settings in the Configuration pane.\nL545: ## Job networking and app-to-app communication\nL546: \nL547: When a job pod starts, sidecar containers (such as the Envoy proxy) are guaranteed to be ready before the main job container begins execution. This ensures that app-to-app calls made by the job at startup succeed without connection failures.\nL548: \nL549: Note\nL550: \nL551: If your job makes calls to other container apps at startup, you don't need to add retry logic for initial sidecar readiness. The platform handles this automatically.\nL552: ## Jobs restrictions\nL553: \nL554: The following features aren't supported:\nL555: \nL556:   * Dapr\nL557:   * Ingress and related features such as custom domains and SSL certificates\nL558: \nL559: ## Next step\nL560: \nL561: cite48†Create a job in Azure Container Apps L562: \nL563: * * *\nL564: \nL565: ## Feedback\nL566: \nL567: Was this page helpful?\nL568: \nL569: Yes No No\nL570: \nL571: Need help with this topic?\nL572: \nL573: Want to try using Ask Learn to clarify or guide you through this topic?\nL574: \nL575: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL576: \nL577: Suggest a fix?\nL578: \nL579: * * *\nL580: \nL581: ## Additional resources\nL582: \nL583: * * *\nL584: \nL585:   * Last updated on 2026-09-16\nL586: ### In this article\nL587: \nL588: Was this page helpful?\nL589: \nL590: Need help with this topic?\nL591: \nL592: Want to try using Ask Learn to clarify or guide you through this topic?\nL593: \nL594: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL595: \nL596: Suggest a fix?\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUse storage mounts in Azure Container Apps | Microsoft Learn (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/container-apps/storage-mounts)\nciteturn57view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Cont